DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Shared/exclusive locks are used to protect data that are read far more
     often than they are written.  Shared/exclusive locks do not implement
     priority propagation like mutexes and reader/writer locks to prevent pri‐
     ority inversions, so shared/exclusive locks should be used prudently.

     Shared/exclusive locks are created with either ssxx__iinniitt() or
     ssxx__iinniitt__ffllaaggss() where _s_x is a pointer to space for a _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_x, and
     _d_e_s_c_r_i_p_t_i_o_n is a pointer to a null-terminated character string that de‐
     scribes the shared/exclusive lock.  The _o_p_t_s argument to ssxx__iinniitt__ffllaaggss()
     specifies a set of optional flags to alter the behavior of _s_x.  It con‐
     tains one or more of the following flags:

     SX_DUPOK      Witness should not log messages about duplicate locks being
                   acquired.

     SX_NOWITNESS  Instruct witness(4) to ignore this lock.

     SX_NOPROFILE  Do not profile this lock.

     SX_RECURSE    Allow threads to recursively acquire exclusive locks for
                   _s_x.

     SX_QUIET      Do not log any operations for this lock via ktr(4).

     SX_NEW        If the kernel has been compiled with ooppttiioonnss IINNVVAARRIIAANNTTSS,
                   ssxx__iinniitt() will assert that the _s_x has not been initialized
                   multiple times without intervening calls to ssxx__ddeessttrrooyy()
                   unless this option is specified.

     Shared/exclusive locks are destroyed with ssxx__ddeessttrrooyy().  The lock _s_x must
     not be locked by any thread when it is destroyed.

     Threads acquire and release a shared lock by calling ssxx__sslloocckk(),
     ssxx__sslloocckk__ssiigg() or ssxx__ttrryy__sslloocckk() and ssxx__ssuunnlloocckk() or ssxx__uunnlloocckk().
     Threads acquire and release an exclusive lock by calling ssxx__xxlloocckk(),
     ssxx__xxlloocckk__ssiigg() or ssxx__ttrryy__xxlloocckk() and ssxx__xxuunnlloocckk() or ssxx__uunnlloocckk().  A
     thread can attempt to upgrade a currently held shared lock to an exclu‐
     sive lock by calling ssxx__ttrryy__uuppggrraaddee().  A thread that has an exclusive
     lock can downgrade it to a shared lock by calling ssxx__ddoowwnnggrraaddee().

     ssxx__ttrryy__sslloocckk() and ssxx__ttrryy__xxlloocckk() will return 0 if the shared/exclusive
     lock cannot be acquired immediately; otherwise the shared/exclusive lock
     will be acquired and a non-zero value will be returned.

     ssxx__ttrryy__uuppggrraaddee() will return 0 if the shared lock cannot be upgraded to
     an exclusive lock immediately; otherwise the exclusive lock will be ac‐
     quired and a non-zero value will be returned.

     ssxx__sslloocckk__ssiigg() and ssxx__xxlloocckk__ssiigg() do the same as their normal versions
     but performing an interruptible sleep.  They return a non-zero value if
     the sleep has been interrupted by a signal or an interrupt, otherwise 0.

     A thread can atomically release a shared/exclusive lock while waiting for
     an event by calling ssxx__sslleeeepp().  For more details on the parameters to
     this function, see sleep(9).

     When compiled with ooppttiioonnss IINNVVAARRIIAANNTTSS and ooppttiioonnss IINNVVAARRIIAANNTT__SSUUPPPPOORRTT, the
     ssxx__aasssseerrtt() function tests _s_x for the assertions specified in _w_h_a_t, and
     panics if they are not met.  One of the following assertions must be
     specified:

     SA_LOCKED    Assert that the current thread has either a shared or an ex‐
                  clusive lock on the _s_x lock pointed to by the first argu‐
                  ment.

     SA_SLOCKED   Assert that the current thread has a shared lock on the _s_x
                  lock pointed to by the first argument.

     SA_XLOCKED   Assert that the current thread has an exclusive lock on the
                  _s_x lock pointed to by the first argument.

     SA_UNLOCKED  Assert that the current thread has no lock on the _s_x lock
                  pointed to by the first argument.

     In addition, one of the following optional assertions may be included
     with either an SA_LOCKED, SA_SLOCKED, or SA_XLOCKED assertion:

     SA_RECURSED     Assert that the current thread has a recursed lock on _s_x.

     SA_NOTRECURSED  Assert that the current thread does not have a recursed
                     lock on _s_x.

     ssxx__xxhhoollddeerr() will return a pointer to the thread which currently holds an
     exclusive lock on _s_x.  If no thread holds an exclusive lock on _s_x, then
     NULL is returned instead.

     ssxx__xxlloocckkeedd() will return non-zero if the current thread holds the exclu‐
     sive lock; otherwise, it will return zero.

     For ease of programming, ssxx__uunnlloocckk() is provided as a macro frontend to
     the respective functions, ssxx__ssuunnlloocckk() and ssxx__xxuunnlloocckk().  Algorithms that
     are aware of what state the lock is in should use either of the two spe‐
     cific functions for a minor performance benefit.

     The SSXX__SSYYSSIINNIITT() macro is used to generate a call to the ssxx__ssyyssiinniitt()
     routine at system startup in order to initialize a given _s_x lock.  The
     parameters are the same as ssxx__iinniitt() but with an additional argument,
     _n_a_m_e, that is used in generating unique variable names for the related
     structures associated with the lock and the sysinit routine.  The
     SSXX__SSYYSSIINNIITT__FFLLAAGGSS() macro can similarly be used to initialize a given _s_x
     lock using ssxx__iinniitt__ffllaaggss().

     A thread may not hold both a shared lock and an exclusive lock on the
     same lock simultaneously; attempting to do so will result in deadlock.

CCOONNTTEEXXTT
     A thread may hold a shared or exclusive lock on an ssxx lock while sleep‐
     ing.  As a result, an ssxx lock may not be acquired while holding a mutex.
     Otherwise, if one thread slept while holding an ssxx lock while another
     thread blocked on the same ssxx lock after acquiring a mutex, then the sec‐
     ond thread would effectively end up sleeping while holding a mutex, which
     is not allowed.

